    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A small humidifier is brilliant at moistening the air around you and almost useless at humidifying a room, and the tank size tells you which one you are buying. Pick by the job: a 3-liter unit for a small room, a tabletop unit for a desk, a bottle-top unit for travel.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small humidifiers are the most crowded and least honest corner of the humidifier market. Hundreds of near-identical units promise to cover a room from a tank the size of a coffee mug, and many of the run-time and coverage figures on listings cannot be squared with the size of the tank. So we started from the numbers that are hard to fudge: tank volume, power source, and the manufacturer's own run-time and coverage ratings, cross-checked against retailer listings and, where one exists, an independent review.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every figure in this guide is a published rating from the maker or a major retailer, and we say so each time. Where sources disagree, which happens constantly with small humidifiers, we tell you which number we trust and why. We did not run our own lab tests on these units, and nothing here should be read as a measurement we made. What we have done is the arithmetic that the listings skip: how much water a tank of a given size can actually put into the air, and what that means for the space it can realistically help.
          </p>

          <p>
            We wanted one strong pick for each way people use a small humidifier. That means a small-room unit that genuinely humidifies an office or a small bedroom, a tabletop model sized for a desk or nightstand, a small evaporative for people with hard water or electronics nearby, a budget pick, a travel unit and a cordless unit. We left out anything with no traceable maker, no published specs, or run-time claims that were impossible for the tank size.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need to humidify a larger space, a small humidifier is the wrong tool and no amount of running it on high will fix that. Our <a href="/best-humidifier/">full humidifier guide</a> covers room and large-room machines, and the sizing logic is laid out in <a href="/what-size-humidifier-do-i-need/">what size humidifier you need</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="personal-zone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Small Humidifier Can and Can't Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most useful thing to understand before buying a small humidifier is the difference between humidifying a room and humidifying the air around you. A room humidifier raises the moisture level of the whole space and holds it there against the dry air leaking in from outside. A personal humidifier puts a plume of moist air near your face, your desk or your pillow, and the room as a whole barely moves.
          </p>

          <p>
            The physics is unforgiving. Take a 150 square foot office with an eight-foot ceiling, about 34 cubic meters of air. At room temperature, lifting that air from 20 percent to 40 percent relative humidity takes roughly 120 grams of water, a little over half a cup, just to charge the air once. Ordinary air leakage then replaces that air, perhaps once an hour or more in an older building, and every exchange carries the moisture away. To hold the room at 40 percent, a humidifier has to keep putting out something in the region of 100 to 150 milliliters an hour. That is a rough estimate with stated assumptions, not a rule, but it explains everything that follows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the same arithmetic against the tanks on this list. A 500 ml cordless unit that lasts 11 hours on low is putting out about 45 ml an hour, which is a third of what the room needs. It will make the air within a few feet of it noticeably softer and leave the room's hygrometer reading where it was. A 3-liter unit like the Levoit Dual 150 running a full day at roughly 125 ml an hour is in the range that can actually move a small closed room. Tank size is the honest proxy for what a small humidifier can do.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes a personal humidifier useless. If your complaint is a dry throat at your desk, dry eyes in front of a monitor, or a parched hotel room for one night, moist air where you are is exactly what you want, and a small unit delivers it without the bulk and refilling of a room machine. Just buy it for that job, and do not expect the room's humidity number to change.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tank size tells you the truth; the coverage claim usually does not.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Divide the tank volume by the run time to get output per hour. Under about 75 ml an hour, treat it as a personal humidifier for the air around you. Over about 120 ml an hour, it can start to hold a small closed room.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

