        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: Desk, Tabletop or Small Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small humidifiers fall into three practical sizes, and matching the size to the job is most of the decision. Personal units, with tanks from a few hundred milliliters up to about a liter, humidify the air around you. Tabletop units, roughly one to two liters, are the desk and nightstand class and can make a difference in a very small, closed room. Compact room units, around three liters and up, are the smallest machines that can hold a small bedroom or home office at a target humidity.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a desk in an open-plan office or a cubicle, a personal or tabletop unit is the right size, because nothing you can fit on a desk will humidify a floor of a building. For a closed home office or a small bedroom under about 200 square feet, a compact room unit like the Levoit Dual 150 is the one that will move the hygrometer. For anything larger, step up to a full-size machine from our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom humidifier guide</a> or the main guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run time is the other sizing number, and it matters more than coverage for most people. A humidifier that runs dry at 3 a.m. or at 2 p.m. is one you will stop using. Work out when you need it, then check that the tank can cover it at the output you will actually run. A 1.5-liter tank that the label says lasts 25 hours on low may last well under half that on the setting a dry January needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, account for the room itself. A closed door, a modern tight window and a room away from the furnace supply all help a small humidifier punch above its weight. An office with a supply vent blowing hot, dry air across the desk, a door that stays open to a big hallway, or a drafty older room will eat a small unit's output. If you are unsure, a cheap hygrometer will tell you within a day whether the unit is making a difference, and it is worth buying one before you decide the humidifier is not working.
          </p>

